verdict:
Not decided. I am fairly sure the per-language criterion holds: by the brief's rule, 1 of 36 in-scope entries needed a per-language edit (S13), or 2 of 36 (5.6%) under the strict reading with U44. Both are under 10%. Only the strictest reading crosses the line, at 16.7%, and that reading counts the type printers and the go printer, which the brief exempts. The claim as worded is that the issues ARE fixed once, and the frozen state does not show that:
- 7 of 36 entries are not fixed, or only partly: X3, S22, U52, U53, V27, U102, and D26 partly.
- All seven sit in shared code the prototype already edited.
- Counted against the proposal they give 22-36%, over the line on every reading.
What would decide it:
- make those 7 shared edits;
- port js, php, csharp, java, kotlin, scala and cpp;
- regenerate NEW and run the fence-fixed checker over all 20 languages, adding code-only and any-wording variants of the regex checks;
- confirm the seven ports add no per-language line beyond S13's testMock.
